  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1142 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe next chance of rain with thunderstorms will be tomorrow,\nespecially later in the day through tomorrow night. Low pressure\nwill gradually shift over the state in the next 24 hours, then\nquickly east of the Divide by Wednesday morning. Precipitation\namounts are expected to be on the lighter side, even with\nstronger showers or storms...with best chances outside of the\ncentral mountains. Totals should remain under 0.10\", with some\npockets up to 0.20\" where stronger development occurs or\nmultiple storms move over the same area. The potential for more\nthan 0.25\" is highest across the southeast and eastern highlands\nat 20-40%. There is a small bullseye across the central\nmountains around Copper Basin as well. Otherwise, chances are\nless than 20%. Otherwise, we are dry today and Wednesday into\nmost of Friday. Low pressure races by to our northwest Friday\nand Saturday, clipping the central mountains. It does look like\nwe COULD see a few showers or storms across the central\nmountains, while the rest of eastern Idaho sees cooler\ntemperatures and gusty winds. On its heels, another area of low\npressure shifts inland. This will keep us on the cooler side of\nthermometer, along with seeing some gusty winds and better \nchance of showers and storms areawide. Confidence is lower on \nwhen this starts, and the Blend of Models is showing the same \nthing. Temperatures Tuesday will be just a couple of degrees \ncooler than today, but we bounce back toward 70s and even \nlow/mid 80s for midweek. We back of a bit with the low and cold\nfront sweeping through Friday through Sunday about 5-10 \ndegrees.",
